    "subject": "Gemini 4 Hydrogen balances accuracy and speed",
    "date": "2026-11-16T18:22:51.000Z",
    "body": "Open source llama.cpp finally reaches version 1.0.0\r\n\r\nDeepLearning.AI Coral Logo  (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN000 )\r\n\r\nData Points\r\n\r\nYour accelerated guide to AI news\r\n\r\nSubscribe (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN001 )    Forward this issue (mailto:?subject=Check%20out%20this%20great%20AI%20newsletter!&body=I%20thought%20you%20might%20be%20interested%20in%20this%20news,%20from%20the%20newest%20issue%20of%20Data%20Points%20by%20DeepLearning.AI:)    Tips/Contact Us (mailto:datapoints@deeplearning.ai?subject=Thoughts%20on%20Data%20Points&body=Here%27s%20some%20feedback%20on%20the%20latest%20issue%20of%20Data%20Points%3A%0A%0A[Your%20feedback%20goes%20here])\r\n\r\nA team observes a screen displaying robot arm movements and data charts in a tech office setting, discussing AI technology. (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN000 )\r\n\r\nGemini 4 Hydrogen balances accuracy and speed\r\n\r\nOpen source llama.cpp finally reaches version 1.0.0\r\n\r\nWelcome back! In today’s edition of Data Points, you’ll learn about our top headlines, and more:\r\n\r\n- Jensen Huang steps down, Colette Kress named interim CEO\r\n- Google releases most promising decision model yet\r\n- Runway’s Praxis-1 leads on robotics simulation benchmark\r\n- Cloud-native developers top 21 million, strain open source\r\n\r\nBut first:\r\n\r\nGoogle debuts a fast model to complement Carbon, replace 3.8 Flash\r\n\r\nGoogle released Gemini 4 Hydrogen, a faster model in the Gemini 4 family than Carbon and Argon, and made it available across AI Studio, the Gemini API, the Gemini app, Search, and Google Antigravity. Hydrogen costs $1.50 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output tokens, flat across a 1 million-token context window, with four thinking levels: low, medium, high (the default), and a new xhigh. Google says Hydrogen scores 51 on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index at the default level (54 at xhigh) against Argon’s 50, and finishes tasks 2.5 times faster than Gemini 3.8 Flash. Google also added Orchestrator to Antigravity, which switches between models such as Carbon and Hydrogen within one session; on Terminal-Bench 4.0 it reached near-Carbon success rates while using 24% less quota and finishing 19% faster, by Google’s measurement. Teams on Gemini 3.8 Flash have a cheaper, faster migration target, and Orchestrator offers a way to split planning and execution across models without managing the routing themselves. (Google (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN002 ) )\r\n\r\nLlama.cpp gets first major update since 2023\r\n\r\nThe llama.cpp team at Hugging Face released v1.0.0 of both libllama and libggml, the project’s first major version after about 3.5 years of development. From now on, breaking changes to the public C API or ABI in each library’s include directory will trigger a major-version bump, while backward-compatible features, backend updates, and model support get minor bumps and bug fixes get patches. The project now ships through Docker, Debian, Homebrew, conda-forge, MacPorts, Winget, and an XCFramework for iOS and macOS, plus pre-release artifacts for Linux, macOS, Windows, Android, and iOS. It supports more than 150 model architecture variants and the mainstream speculative decoding methods, and runs CI on self-hosted runners with hardware support from NVIDIA, AMD, Qualcomm, Arm, IBM, and Intel. Developers who link against libllama or libggml can now treat the version number as a signal for when an upgrade will break their build, and can pin to a major version in package managers. (Hugging Face (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN003 ) )\r\n\r\nNvidia CEO and founder Jensen Huang resigns\r\n\r\nFollowing his admission of an extramarital affair and a rapid third-party investigation, Nvidia announced that Jensen Huang resigned as president, CEO, and board member, effective immediately, after 33 years at the company. CFO Colette Kress is now interim CEO while keeping her CFO role, and the board has begun a search that will consider internal and external candidates. Harvey C. Jones, a former Synopsys CEO, was appointed the board’s first-ever chair. The company says operations, customer commitments, and the product roadmap will continue without interruption, and the third-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings report stays scheduled for November 17. (Nvidia (https://info.deeplearning.ai/e3t/Ctc/LX+113/cJhC404/ANONTOKEN004 ) )\r\n\r\nGoogle releases decision model that rivals Jev at higher price\r\n\r\nGoogle released the Gemini Decisions API, backed by a new model, gemini-decision-1, that returns calibrated probabilities for typed questions instead of generating text. One request takes a shared input (text, image, audio, video, or PDF) and up to 1,000 questions, each a yes/no, a choice among 2 to 255 options, or a score on a rubric of up to 255 levels. Input costs $0.10 per 1M tokens, output tokens are always zero, and the shared input is billed once per request; Google reports 150-250 ms end-to-end latency for inputs up to 16K tokens. In Google’s self-reported benchmarks, the model leads 7 of 9 accuracy tests and has a top-1 calibration error of 0.0250, about half the 0.0494 of TypeSafe Jev1.14, but it trails Jev 1.14 on When2Call (79.9% vs 82.0%) and Mind2Web Multimodal (86.2% vs 86.8%) and costs more per token ($0.10 vs $0.042). It runs only on Google’s hosted API, with a 262,144-token context and no prompt caching or Batch API. For agent builders, this replaces generate-then-parse for routing, policy checks and grading with numeric outputs they can branch on. Note that the benchmark figures are unverified and repeated context is billed in full on every call. (MarkTechPost (https:/",
